#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172707} :activity/id #uuid "94fbade8-75e0-5892-a2c1-a199c318e464" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050388 :topic/id #uuid "448cb9b4-3610-522d-b3fa-8e841dae3795" :topic/math-academy-id 3411} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173279 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to prove universal statements that involve the evenness and oddness of simple expressions. \n\nFirst, we have the following definitions:\n\n- Two integers have the **same parity** if they're *both* even or *both* odd.\n\n- Two integers have **opposite parity** if *one* of them is even and the *other* is odd.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The integers $2$ and $4$ have the *same* parity because they're both even.\n\n- The integers $1$ and $7$ have the *same* parity because they're both odd.\n\n- On the other hand, the integers $1$ and $6$ have *opposite* parity because $1$ is odd and $6$ is even." :tutorial/id #uuid "c4aa6851-79bf-5e1e-a33e-0dfd2ad496cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8323 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "14ad55b6-7892-5dfa-9436-2d3d20ff4228" :step/math-academy-id 153995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173293 :tutorial/content "The list below gives the first few even integers:\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\pm 2, \\quad \\pm 4, \\quad \\pm 6, \\quad \\pm 8, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nAn integer is even if it is a multiple of $2.$ And, since every integer is a multiple of its factors, every even integer can be written as \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2\\times 0 \\\\ 2 & =2\\times 1 \\\\ 4 & =2\\times 2 \\\\ 12 & =2\\times 6 \\\\ -24 & =2\\times (-12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose we know that an integer $n$ is even. Then, we can rewrite $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThis choice of letter for the second factor is arbitrary: we chose ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ here, but we can use any letter we like! However, the following variables are commonly used to denote integers:\n\n$$\na, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad l, \\quad m, \\quad n, \\quad p, \\quad q, \\quad r, \\quad x, \\quad y, \\quad z\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dda29ab5-09f0-5127-a32f-a373f3ff4735" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9213 :tutorial/title "Representing Even Integers"} :step/id #uuid "235f8ba9-1310-5258-a3ba-71ffd2aec467" :step/math-academy-id 153996 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173292 :tutorial/content "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 8n-4$ *is even.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that $8n-4$ can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}},$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThe first step is to factor out $2$ from the expression.\n\n$$\n8n-4 = 2(4n-2)\n$$\n\nNext, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 4n-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z,$ we have that $4n-2\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z.$ It's essential to state this since the whole argument breaks down if the second factor is sometimes not an integer. \n\nNow, our expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(4n-2) = 2{\\color{blue}{a}}.\n$$\n\nSo, since $8n-4$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $8n-4$ is even *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z.$\n\nLet's take a look at another example. Before we do, note that statements about parity are often written informally, and so the universal quantifier $\\forall$ is usually hidden." :tutorial/id #uuid "de5edb60-1718-5837-893e-d851254ba835" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9212 :tutorial/title "Showing an Expression Is Even"} :step/id #uuid "cde089ce-129c-5cde-a6ff-9bbd9bd6e10f" :step/math-academy-id 153997 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171931 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171737 :question/id #uuid "e0de7c98-f98c-516a-8636-98eb63d36f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13305"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba331138-93b8-5fb0-918b-17dc6d3e4029" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406665}] :knowledge-point/title "Showing a Linear Expression Is Even"} :step/id #uuid "3b954d4a-b9f7-556f-8f1c-56ece4c5ad13" :step/math-academy-id 153998 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173280 :tutorial/content "Recall that an integer is *odd* if it is not even. The list below gives the first few odd integers:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1, \\quad \\pm 3, \\quad \\pm 5, \\quad \\pm 7, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that every odd integer can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}} + 1.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2\\times 0+1 \\\\ 3 & =2\\times 1+1 \\\\ 5 & =2\\times 2+1 \\\\ 19 & =2\\times 9+1 \\\\ -17 & =2\\times (-9)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, an integer $n$ is odd if it can be written as \n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}+1\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}a$ is an integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5cbfaed-ec7d-5a59-8353-d76d1e8f347f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8324 :tutorial/title "Representing Odd Integers"} :step/id #uuid "13224a62-ad5e-5b92-af3c-f7d1814d2cce" :step/math-academy-id 154001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173294 :tutorial/content "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 4n+7$ *is odd.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that it can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1,$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThe first step is to rewrite the expression by isolating $+1.$ We can do this by decomposing $7$ into $6+1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4n+7 = 4n+6+1 = (4n+6)+1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $2$ from the expression in parentheses:\n\n$$\n(4n+6)+1 = 2(2n+3) + 1\n$$\n\nThen, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 2n+3.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z,$ we have that $2n+3\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z.$ \n\nThis means our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(2n+3) + 1= 2{\\color{blue}{a}} + 1.\n$$\n\nSo, since $4n+7$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $4n+7$ is odd *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a08c4d48-b406-5e71-af14-3924b9d0b7eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9214 :tutorial/title "Showing a Statement Is Odd"} :step/id #uuid "7b5aa962-25ba-564b-808e-06e699aa1936" :step/math-academy-id 154002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171932 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171738 :question/id #uuid "9e1e05cf-2b3a-5189-b913-06ba1a19d88e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13306"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4090596b-29f5-5dcc-93f8-ff30727e250b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172424}] :knowledge-point/title "Showing a Linear Expression Is Odd"} :step/id #uuid "0c89fd07-6c91-536c-a727-60ec0d4f7465" :step/math-academy-id 154003 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171933 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171735 :question/id #uuid "2423fe12-360a-5bda-9492-e3ed5f95435d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13300"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ee426ac-48be-52e8-a0bb-b48b9087cf97" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172418}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Parity of Non-Linear Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "eebb15e9-add4-5789-8d61-3bc25584f094" :step/math-academy-id 154006 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171934 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171736 :question/id #uuid "00e763aa-c56c-5201-ae3b-9a4cec1af87d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13302"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd14b427-db87-5c61-bcd2-4ecec58450a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172420}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Parity of Non-Linear Expressions With Multiple Variables"} :step/id #uuid "646f2c13-6b17-500a-a791-ae4da9c7b806" :step/math-academy-id 154009}] :activity/title "Parity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172200}]}
